  "query_preview": "..(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 525 AM EDT WED JUL 22 2026\n\nAt the onset of the long term period, upper level troughing will \nextend from eastern Canada into the northeast CONUS, with an \nadditional area of troughing associated with a closed upper level \nlow located off the Pacific Northwest coast. Broad ridging is\nexpected to be centered from the Southern Plains to southern NM\narea, with anomalously high 500 mb heights across much of the \ncentral and western CONUS. Locally, eastern Kentucky will find \nitself in a region of northwesterly flow aloft, with the forecast\narea sandwiched between ridging and troughing. At the surface, an\narea of high pressure will begin the period centered form the\nSouthern Great Lakes to the Northeast Conus, though an inverted\ntrough may extend into pats of eastern KY/Southern Appalachian\nregion from a wave of low pressure traversing a stalled boundary\nacross the Gulf coast states. With drier air in place and the high\nto the north and northeast generally remaining in control, lows \nThursday night should reach the mid and upper 50s in valleys and \nnorthern sections to the low 60s south and on coalfield ridges. \nTypical fog formation is also probable along the larger creeks, \nrivers, and lakes.\n\nChances for convection will return Friday afternoon mainly to\nareas near the VA and TN borders. These chances are where more \nsubstantial moisture will be present near and east of the inverted\ntrough that lingers in parts of the Southern Appalachians as a \nshortwave embedded within persistent northwesterly flow aloft \nrotates into our area. Upper level ridging should remain generally\npersistent across parts of the western Conus to Plains over the \nweekend and into early next week with an upper trough from Canada \ninto parts of the eastern Conus. Northwest flow aloft should \npersist, and with the potential for additional embedded impulses, \nrain chances will continue through Saturday, although the timing \nand details of this activity will need to be refined in later \nforecast periods. By Sunday, the eastern Canada to \neastern/northeastern Conus trough will deepen along the Atlantic \nCoast, with flow aloft becoming increasingly northerly over the \nforecast area. Model spread increases significantly at this point,\nwith rain chances dependent on embedded impulses remain difficult\nto resolve this far out. For now, slight to chance PoPs are \nforecast through the end of the period, generally peaking each \nafternoon and evening though uncertainty in timing and coverage \nremains high at this time.",
